vfs: allow recursive bind mounts of nsfs files (upstream PR 16872)

What: mount(2) with MS_BIND|MS_REC of a namespace file that is not in the
caller's mount namespace, such as /proc/<pid>/task/<tid>/ns/net, binds the
file, as Linux's __do_loopback() does (copy_tree() with CL_COPY_MNT_NS_FILE),
instead of failing with EINVAL; cgroupfs and cgroup2fs stay limited to
non-recursive binds. With it come the checks Linux makes when a mount
namespace file is bound (mnt_ns_loop(): only into an older mount namespace)
or met by a recursive copy.

Why: Podman pins the network namespace of each container it gives a network
of its own with mount("/proc/<pid>/task/<tid>/ns/net",
"/run/netns/netns-<id>", MS_BIND|MS_REC|MS_SHARED). In a gVisor sandbox every
such container failed: "failed to bind mount ns at /run/netns/netns-...:
invalid argument". Only --network host worked.

Upstream: this is google/gvisor PR 16872 (head 3dd57a3ffbc7, open on
2026-11-17), applied unchanged. Drop this patch once the gVisor release ALE
builds from has it.

Verified:
- The PR's syscall tests MountTest.BindMountNamespaceFile and
  NetworkNamespaceTest.RecursiveBindMount, built in gVisor's builder container
  and run as root with CAP_NET_ADMIN, CAP_NET_RAW and CAP_SYS_ADMIN, pass on
  Linux 7.0 under runc and on runsc with this patch, under the runtime
  arguments of runsc and of runsc-nested, and fail on ALE's 0.1.2. The whole
  mount_test (116) and network_namespace_test (10) pass on runsc with it but
  MountTest.UserNamespaceSysfsMountOverEmptyDir, which fails on 0.1.2 and on
  Linux 7.0 too.
- Podman 4.9.3 in an Ubuntu 24.04 sandbox under runsc-nested: podman run on
  its default network created the network namespace, and failed next in the
  CNI bridge plugin (patch 0011) or in netavark (patch 0012).

